    │ 
    │ Note 1: computing both sides gives `List.foldLeft (acc o => (match …) acc o) 0 xs` and `List.foldLeft (acc o => (match …) acc o) 0 xs`
    │ 
    │ Note 2: `rfl` proves `a = b` only when both sides compute to the same value
    │ 
    │ Note 3: `(match …)` is a `match` written inside `foo` and `bar`, marked "this `match`" above; the compiler turned it into a function that source can't name, and writing the same `match` again makes a different one. To state a lemma about it, make it a local `def` in its arm and refer to it as `owner.name`, where `owner` is the definition it is written in (docs/syntax.md, "Proving things about a definition with an inner `match`")
    │ 
    │ Note 4: two different `(match …)` appear here, which print alike but are different hidden functions, and two such functions are never equal, even when written the same; to prove something about both, name the step as a `def` and use it in both places
